</ul><h1>Sleep Tight</h1><p>A white noise machine for your baby? That also provides soothing light? And it’s wi-fi enabled, so you can adjust the settings to your kiddo’s exact liking and connect to an optional subscription service replete with lullabies and bedtime stories?</p>
<p>Sounds soothing!</p>
<p>And by all accounts, it is. On Walmart’s site, it gets a very nice 4.5 out of 5 based on over 8,000 ratings.</p>
<p>And yet…</p>
<p>The user-submitted photos over there showing this thing in action (if you can describe a light being on as ‘in action’) can look pretty friggin’ spooky!</p>
<p>Which is why, today, we’re ranking four Walmart review photos based on their creepiness:</p>
<ol>
<li></li>
</ol>
<p><img height="766" src="https://d2b8wt72ktn9a2.cloudfront.net/mediocre/image/upload/v1780587716/usdsvgihtqohknwxufrx.png" width="570" /></p>
<p>A toy that comes to life and makes terrifying mischief is a horror trope as old as time. But this photo isn’t giving those vibes. The light is warm, and Big Bird’s eyes are too sweet.</p>
<p><strong>Creepiness rating</strong>: 2/10</p>
<hr />
